---
import Callout from "@luciodale/docs-ui-kit/components/Callout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "@luciodale/docs-ui-kit/components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Comparison from "@luciodale/docs-ui-kit/components/Comparison.astro";
import ContentSection from "@luciodale/docs-ui-kit/components/ContentSection.astro";
import InlineCode from "@luciodale/docs-ui-kit/components/InlineCode.astro";
import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro";
import { href } from "../../lib/link";

export const prerender = true;
---

<DocsPage
	title="react-socket vs react-use-websocket"
	description="How react-socket compares to react-use-websocket. Honest trade-offs on type safety, subscriptions, in-flight tracking, and offline queuing for React WebSocket clients."
>
	<ContentSection label="The choice">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			<InlineCode>react-use-websocket</InlineCode> is the default pick when
			you reach for a WebSocket hook in React. It wraps the browser
			<InlineCode>WebSocket</InlineCode> API into a clean
			<InlineCode>useWebSocket</InlineCode> hook, handles reconnection, and
			exposes
			<InlineCode>sendMessage</InlineCode> / <InlineCode>lastMessage</InlineCode>.
			For simple realtime views it's more than enough.
		</p>
		<p class="text-white/60 leading-relaxed">
			react-socket sits one floor up. It's still unopinionated about your
			protocol or state, but it orchestrates the things you start to
			hand-roll the moment your app gets real: typed messages, ref counted
			subscriptions shared across components, in-flight tracking with ack
			IDs, offline queuing, and a dev inspector. If you're building a chat
			room, a trading UI, or a streaming LLM client, that's where the
			boilerplate eats weeks.
		</p>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Where each one sits">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			Libraries that do <em>less</em> than react-socket leave you to
			implement subscription dedup, in-flight tracking, offline replay, and
			type propagation yourself. Libraries that do <em>more</em> pick a
			protocol (STOMP, GraphQL subscriptions, Phoenix Channels) and lock
			you to it.
		</p>
		<p class="text-white/60 leading-relaxed">
			react-use-websocket sits firmly in the "less" camp: a thin, friendly
			hook around the browser primitive. react-socket stays
			protocol-agnostic but handles the transport plumbing around the
			socket, so your app code stays focused on what the messages
			<em>mean</em>, not how they arrive.
		</p>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Feature by feature">
		<Comparison
			columns={[
				{
					name: "@luciodale/react-socket",
					href: "https://www.npmjs.com/package/@luciodale/react-socket",
					tagline: "Orchestrator, protocol agnostic",
					highlight: true,
				},
				{
					name: "react-use-websocket",
					href: "https://www.npmjs.com/package/react-use-websocket",
					tagline: "Thin hook around browser WebSocket",
				},
			]}
			rows={[
				{
					feature: "Typed message schemas",
					description:
						"Client and server union types flow through send, receive, serialize, deserialize.",
					values: ["yes", "no"],
				},
				{
					feature: "Automatic reconnection",
					description:
						"Exponential backoff with jitter, configurable caps.",
					values: ["yes", "yes"],
				},
				{
					feature: "Ref counted subscriptions",
					description:
						"Multiple components subscribe to the same channel with a single server message.",
					values: ["yes", "no"],
				},
				{
					feature: "In-flight tracking with ack IDs",
					description:
						"Tag a send, mark it pending, hear when the server confirms or when the connection drops before delivery.",
					values: ["yes", "no"],
				},
				{
					feature: "Offline message queue",
					description:
						"Sends while disconnected persist and flush on reconnect. Pluggable storage.",
					values: ["yes", "no"],
				},
				{
					feature: "Keep alive ping / pong",
					description:
						"Detects silent servers before the browser does and triggers a reconnect.",
					values: ["yes", "partial"],
				},
				{
					feature: "DevTools inspector",
					description:
						"Drop in component that visualizes traffic, subscriptions, and in-flight state.",
					values: ["yes", "no"],
				},
				{
					feature: "Pluggable transport",
					description:
						"Swap the browser WebSocket for any IWebSocketTransport. Useful for tests and non-browser runtimes.",
					values: ["yes", "no"],
				},
				{
					feature: "API shape",
					values: [
						{ text: "Manager class + hooks" },
						{ text: "Single hook" },
					],
				},
				{
					feature: "Bundle size",
					values: [{ text: "~5 kB gzipped" }, { text: "~3 kB gzipped" }],
				},
			]}
			caption="Feature comparison between react-socket and react-use-websocket"
		/>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Type safety in practice">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			With react-use-websocket, <InlineCode>lastJsonMessage</InlineCode> is
			<InlineCode>unknown</InlineCode> and <InlineCode>sendJsonMessage</InlineCode>
			accepts any JSON-serializable value. You either cast everywhere or build a
			wrapper that enforces your protocol. Fine for a toy, brittle for an app.
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			code={`// react-use-websocket: types stop at the hook boundary
const { sendJsonMessage, lastJsonMessage } = useWebSocket(url);

sendJsonMessage({ action: "ecoh", text: "hi" });  // typo compiles
const msg = lastJsonMessage as ServerMsg;          // trust me bro`}
			language="tsx"
			filename="use-websocket.tsx"
		/>
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
			With react-socket, <InlineCode>WebSocketManager&lt;TClientMsg, TServerMsg&gt;</InlineCode>
			propagates your protocol through every callback. Rename a field and the compiler
			lists the callsites that need to change.
		</p>
		<CodeBlock
			code={`// react-socket: types flow end to end
const manager = new WebSocketManager<TClientMsg, TServerMsg>({
  url,
  serialize: (msg) => JSON.stringify(msg),
  deserialize: (raw) => JSON.parse(raw) as TServerMsg,
  onMessageReceived(msg) {
    // msg is TServerMsg, fully narrowed by switch on action
  },
});

manager.send({ data: { action: "ecoh", text: "hi" } }); // compile error`}
			language="tsx"
			filename="react-socket.tsx"
		/>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Subscriptions without the bookkeeping">
		<p class="text-white/60 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
			Anything beyond a chat demo eventually needs many components reading
			the same stream: ten price tickers for the same symbol, two avatars
			and a header all watching the same presence channel. With a raw hook
			you either lift state and share, or you accept that subscribes and
			unsubscribes fire per mount, which floods the server.
		</p>
		<p class="text-white/60 leading-relaxed">
			react-socket ref counts subscriptions by a stable key. The subscribe
			payload goes out on the first mount. The unsubscribe fires on the
			last unmount. Five components, one server message. Reconnect and
			every active subscription restores itself without you writing any
			code.
		</p>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="When to pick react-use-websocket">
		<Callout type="info" title="Pick it when">
			You need a single socket, a single component, and the browser default
			behavior is enough. Short-lived dashboards, toy demos, and features
			where a dropped message or two doesn't matter. If your whole
			WebSocket usage fits in one hook call, react-use-websocket is the
			right size.
		</Callout>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="When to pick react-socket">
		<Callout type="tip" title="Pick it when">
			You have a real protocol with multiple message types, components
			sharing channels, sends that must survive a disconnect, or a chat or
			trading or LLM streaming UX where dropped messages corrupt state.
			Pick it when you've already written
			<InlineCode>ref counted subscriptions</InlineCode> or <InlineCode>in-flight tracking</InlineCode>
			by hand once and don't want to do it again.
		</Callout>
	</ContentSection>

	<ContentSection label="Next steps">
		<ul class="space-y-2 text-white/60">
			<li>
				<a href={href("/docs/getting-started")} class="text-accent hover:underline">Getting started</a> — install and wire up the manager
			</li>
			<li>
				<a href={href("/docs/subscriptions")} class="text-accent hover:underline">Subscriptions</a> — how ref counting actually works
			</li>
			<li>
				<a href={href("/docs/optimistic-updates")} class="text-accent hover:underline">Sending messages</a> — ack IDs and in-flight tracking
			</li>
		</ul>
	</ContentSection>
</DocsPage>
